\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 02 \'b7 The Problem\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\i\b0\fs32 \cf0 Asset (mode dark):
\f1\i0  02-1.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Colour contrast of the severity chip, alert notice, and outlined button in dark mode\

\f2\i Asset (mode light):
\f1\i0  02-2.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Colour contrast of the severity chip, alert notice, and outlined button in light mode\
The audit turned up three things that looked like separate problems.\
Colour hadn\'92t been assigned so much as accumulated. No rule governed what any colour meant. The severity chip (1) held up best: colour-coded with a label, passing AA in both modes. The alert notice (2) and outlined button (3) passed AA in light but failed in dark. The dark value had been set once by eye, not derived alongside its light counterpart.\

\f2\i Asset:
\f1\i0  02-3.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Icons with inconsistent styling\
Icons came from more than one library at more than one size \'97 sharp corners beside rounded ones, a set that read as assembled, not designed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 All three were the same fault: colour, mode, and iconography decided per screen instead of assigned by role from one library.
\f1\b0  The product looked assembled, not designed, because that\'92s how it had been put together.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa398\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 03 \'b7 Constraints\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Systematizing a changing product.
\f1\b0  The system was built alongside the redesign, not before it, so the rules had to hold for screens that were still moving.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The system had to be legible to code, not just to designers.
\f1\b0  Figma variables were going into the codebase as tokens, so the structure had to work for the developers building with it.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Three designers, one system.
\f1\b0  Whatever I built had to work for colleagues immediately, not just me.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Data density was not negotiable.
\f1\b0  The tables hold guards, alerts and threats, everything a responder triages.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa398\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 04 \'b7 The Decisions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa373\partightenfactor0

\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf0 1 \'97 A palette was the start, not the answer\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\i\b0\fs32 \cf0 Asset:
\f1\i0  04-1-1.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Example button background token path\

\f2\i Asset:
\f1\i0  04-1-2.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Button intent and saliency\
The palette was raw material, and a semantic layer went on top, defined together with Guardrail\'92s front-end developer.\
Every token is described by:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Where it\'92s used (surface, component, input)\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Its intent (primary, secondary, plain, positive, warning, negative)\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 How much attention it draws (high, medium, low saliency)\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 How it\'92s applied (background, border, text, icon)\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Its state (default, hover, pressed, focus, disabled)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf0 A full path might read component / secondary / high / background / default, the default background for a CTA in the secondary brand colour, weighted to draw the eye first.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 Asset:
\f1\i0  04-1-3.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Alert notice colours in light and dark mode\
Mode became a further axis of the same taxonomy, not a separate pass. Every token holds a light and dark value defined and checked together. Each value is verified against AA, so a failing token is caught before it reaches a screen.\
The cost is that the taxonomy has to be learned before it\'92s used: five dimensions, long names, and a designer who wants one colour has to answer several questions to get it. 
\f0\b Harder to use casually, much harder to use incorrectly.
\f1\b0  AAA was the aim on text, but reaching 7:1 would have narrowed the palette, so some settled for AA instead, keeping the range the product needed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa373\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf0 2 \'97 Two views, not one\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\i\b0\fs32 \cf0 Asset:
\f1\i0  04-2-1.mov\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Prototype of table and list view on mobile\
The typical answer for a dense table on a small screen is to replace it with stacked cards or hidden columns. 
\f0\b I kept the table, horizontal scroll and all, and added a list view beside it.
\f1\b0 \
Tables exist because comparison across columns is the task. Collapse it and you've removed what the table was for, not made it mobile. However, at a phone width a table is genuinely hard to read, so the list view covers other needs that aren't comparison.\
The cost is two views to maintain, and a decision the viewer now has to make, minimized by the breakpoints:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Above 768px: table only, no button group.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 640\'96768px: the button group appears, defaulting to table.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Below 640px: the same button group defaults to list. The option never disappears; someone who came to compare can still switch to table, they just have to do so.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

